The attached image, corresponding to Fig. 4 in the Journal of Controlled Release 2015 article, illustrates the uptake of antigen and Nano-11 by bone marrow-derived dendritic cells (BMDCs). Confocal microscopy shows that Nano-11 (red) and ovalbumin (OVA, green) are internalized by BMDCs. Notably, when OVA is adsorbed to Nano-11, the uptake of OVA is significantly enhanced and both Nano-11 and OVA co-localize within the cells. This demonstrates Nano-11's ability to efficiently deliver negatively charged protein antigens into dendritic cells, a critical step for initiating an effective immune response as a vaccine adjuvant.